1. Start with the most important idea: what does it mean for AI to "learn"?
Imagine you give a model thousands of examples:
Input → correct answer
For example:
House size → house price
1000 sq ft → ₹40 lakh
1500 sq ft → ₹60 lakh
2000 sq ft → ₹80 lakh
The model initially doesn't know the relationship.
It makes a prediction.
Then we ask:
"How wrong were you?"
That error is represented by a loss function.
The model then adjusts its internal numbers called parameters/weights.
It repeats this process thousands or millions of times.
So:
Data → Prediction → Loss → Gradients → Parameter update → Prediction again
That's the fundamental learning loop.
________________________________________
2. Neural networks
A neural network is essentially a collection of mathematical operations arranged in layers.
A very simplified neuron does:
inputs → weighted sum → activation → output
For example:
x1 ──┐
     │
x2 ──┼──> [Neuron] ──> output
     │
x3 ──┘
The neuron has:
•	weights → importance given to inputs 
•	bias → adjustable offset 
•	activation function → introduces non-linearity 
A simple equation is:
z = w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + ... + b
Then:
output = activation(z)
Multiple neurons form a layer.
Multiple layers form a neural network.
Why multiple layers?
Because different layers can learn different levels of patterns.
For example, in image recognition:
Pixels
 ↓
Edges
 ↓
Shapes
 ↓
Parts
 ↓
Object
The network itself learns useful representations during training rather than us manually specifying every feature.
This is one of the major ideas emphasized by 3Blue1Brown's neural-network series. 
________________________________________
3. Gradient descent — how the model improves
Suppose your model predicts:
Actual = 100
but predicts:
Predicted = 70
There is an error.
The model needs to change its weights so that next time the prediction moves closer to 100.
But there may be millions or billions of parameters.
So the question becomes:
Which parameters should change, and by how much?
That's where gradients come in.
A gradient tells us how the loss changes when a parameter changes.
Then gradient descent updates parameters approximately like:
new weight = old weight − learning rate × gradient
Learning rate
The learning rate controls the size of the update.
Too large
   ↓
overshooting / unstable learning

Good
   ↓
steady improvement

Too small
   ↓
very slow learning
3Blue1Brown's playlist specifically covers cost functions, training and gradient descent. 
________________________________________
4. Backpropagation
This is one of the most important concepts.
Backpropagation calculates how much each parameter contributed to the error.
It uses the chain rule from calculus to efficiently calculate gradients throughout the network.
Think:
INPUT
  ↓
Layer 1
  ↓
Layer 2
  ↓
Layer 3
  ↓
Prediction
  ↓
LOSS
Backpropagation goes in the opposite direction:
LOSS
 ↑
Layer 3
 ↑
Layer 2
 ↑
Layer 1
It determines how the loss changes with respect to the parameters.
Important distinction
Don't say:
"Backpropagation updates the weights."
More precisely:
Backpropagation calculates gradients.
The optimizer uses those gradients to update weights.
That's an excellent interview point.
________________________________________
5. What exactly does a model learn?
This is where AI Foundations becomes interesting.
A model doesn't store a simple list like:
Paris = capital of France
2 + 2 = 4
Dog = animal
Instead, information is represented through patterns distributed across parameters and internal representations.
For a neural network:
parameters + architecture + learned representations
work together to produce predictions.
And importantly:
A model's behavior is not necessarily the same thing as human understanding.
A model can produce an excellent answer without possessing human-like understanding or consciousness.
________________________________________
6. From normal neural networks to LLMs
Now let's move to language.
An LLM = Large Language Model.
Hugging Face describes LLMs as a powerful subset of NLP models characterized by large scale, extensive training data, and the ability to perform many language tasks. 
The basic pipeline is:
Human text
    ↓
Tokenization
    ↓
Token IDs
    ↓
Embeddings
    ↓
Transformer
    ↓
Probabilities
    ↓
Next token
    ↓
More tokens
    ↓
Text
Understanding this pipeline is extremely important.
________________________________________
7. What is a token?
An LLM doesn't directly process English sentences like humans do.
It processes tokens.
A token can be:
•	a complete word 
•	part of a word 
•	punctuation 
•	a number 
•	a special symbol 
For example, a sentence may conceptually become:
"I love machine learning"
        ↓
["I", " love", " machine", " learning"]
The exact tokenization depends on the tokenizer.
The tokens are then converted into numerical IDs.
For example:
"I"       → 40
"love"    → 231
"machine" → 1842
Those numbers are then mapped to vectors called embeddings.
Karpathy's tokenizer lecture emphasizes that tokenization is its own stage, with its own training process and encode/decode operations, and discusses Byte Pair Encoding (BPE). 
________________________________________
8. Why can't we just give the whole sentence to the model?
Because neural networks work with numerical representations.
So:
"I love AI"
becomes something like:
[Token 1, Token 2, Token 3]
then:
[vector 1, vector 2, vector 3]
These vectors contain numerical representations that the Transformer can manipulate.
________________________________________
9. Transformers — the heart of modern LLMs
The Transformer architecture was introduced in 2017 and became the foundation of many modern language models. 
The most important idea inside it is:
Attention
Suppose:
"The animal didn't cross the road because it was tired."
What does "it" refer to?
The model needs context.
Attention allows a token to consider other relevant tokens.
Very simplified:
The animal didn't cross the road because it was tired
     ↑                          ↑
     └──────── attention ───────┘
The model can learn which pieces of context are important for interpreting each token.
________________________________________
10. Self-attention
In self-attention, tokens in the same sequence interact with one another.
The famous conceptual components are:
Query
What information am I looking for?
Key
What information do I contain?
Value
What information should I provide?
The model compares queries and keys to calculate relevance.
Then it uses those relevance scores to combine values.
Conceptually:
Query
  ↓
Compare with Keys
  ↓
Attention scores
  ↓
Softmax
  ↓
Weighted Values
  ↓
New representation
The mathematical form is:
Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(QKᵀ / √dₖ)V
You don't need to blindly memorize this yet.
Understand the story:
Find relevant tokens → give them more attention → combine their information.
3Blue1Brown's Transformer and attention lessons focus heavily on visualizing this process. 
________________________________________
11. Why "self" attention?
Because the sequence attends to itself.
For example:
I love machine learning
The representation of "learning" can use information from:
I
love
machine
learning
rather than treating each word independently.
________________________________________
12. Multi-head attention
One attention mechanism can learn one type of relationship.
But language contains many relationships.
So Transformers use multiple attention heads.
Conceptually:
                 ┌── Head 1 → relationship A
Input → Attention├── Head 2 → relationship B
                 ├── Head 3 → relationship C
                 └── Head 4 → relationship D
                         ↓
                    Combined
Different heads can learn different kinds of relationships.
________________________________________
13. What else is inside a Transformer?
A simplified Transformer block contains:
Input
 ↓
Self-Attention
 ↓
Residual connection
 ↓
Layer Normalization
 ↓
Feed-Forward Network
 ↓
Residual connection
 ↓
Layer Normalization
 ↓
Output
Important components:
Self-attention
Handles relationships between tokens.
Feed-forward network / MLP
Performs additional nonlinear transformations on representations.
Residual connections
Help information and gradients flow through deep networks.
Layer normalization
Helps stabilize the network.
Positional information
Provides information about token order because attention itself doesn't inherently encode sequence position.
Hugging Face's Transformer explanation covers these architectural ideas and distinguishes encoder-only, decoder-only and encoder-decoder systems. 
