14. GPT — how does it actually learn language?
This is one of the most beautiful ideas.
GPT-style models are generally decoder-only Transformers.
Their basic training task is:
Predict the next token.
Suppose the text is:
The sky is
The model predicts probabilities:
blue      0.70
beautiful 0.10
dark      0.06
...
Then the actual next token is compared with the prediction.
The loss tells the model how wrong it was.
Then its parameters are updated.
Next example.
Next batch.
Millions/billions of tokens.
Repeated again and again.
Eventually the model becomes extremely good at predicting language patterns.
Karpathy's GPT-from-scratch lecture demonstrates this progression starting with a simple bigram model and moving toward self-attention, multi-head attention and a complete Transformer. 
________________________________________
15. Why can next-token prediction produce surprisingly capable models?
Because predicting the next token requires learning many patterns.
To predict:
"The capital of France is ___"
the model benefits from learning relationships involving:
•	language 
•	syntax 
•	facts 
•	context 
•	common phrases 
•	concepts 
•	relationships between entities 
So the training objective is simple:
predict the next token
but the internal patterns needed to perform that task can become extremely rich.
________________________________________
16. Pretraining vs fine-tuning
Pretraining
Start with randomly initialized parameters and train on a huge corpus.
Huge dataset
     ↓
Pretraining
     ↓
General pretrained model
This can require enormous amounts of compute and data.
Fine-tuning
Start with an existing pretrained model.
Pretrained model
       ↓
Task-specific data
       ↓
Fine-tuning
       ↓
Specialized model
For example:
A general language model
↓
medical-domain training data
↓
medical-domain model
Fine-tuning is much cheaper than training from scratch because the model already has useful learned representations. 
________________________________________
17. What happens when you type a prompt?
Suppose you ask:
"Explain photosynthesis."
The system roughly does:
Prompt
 ↓
Tokenizer
 ↓
Token IDs
 ↓
Embeddings
 ↓
Transformer
 ↓
Logits
 ↓
Probabilities
 ↓
Select next token
 ↓
Add token to context
 ↓
Predict next token
 ↓
Repeat
The model generates the response autoregressively.
That means:
Each newly generated token becomes part of the context used to generate the next token.
Hugging Face describes inference as this two-phase process:
Prefill → Decode
where the prompt is processed first, followed by sequential token generation. 
________________________________________
18. Does the model "look up" every answer?
Not necessarily.
A pretrained language model's parameters contain learned statistical representations from training.
Without external retrieval/tools, it generally isn't performing a database search for every answer.
That's one reason it can answer instantly but also sometimes produce incorrect information.
