20. What AI CANNOT reliably do
This is equally important as knowing what AI can do.
AI does not guarantee:
100% factual accuracy
A model can confidently produce something false.
Perfect reasoning
A model can solve one difficult problem and fail on a seemingly simple variation.
Perfect mathematics
LLMs can make arithmetic or logical mistakes.
Perfect current knowledge
Without an appropriate current information source, a model's knowledge can be outdated.
Zero bias
Models learn from data, and data can contain biases.
Human-level understanding
Good language generation doesn't automatically prove human-like understanding or consciousness.
Guaranteed consistency
The same question can sometimes produce different answers depending on generation settings/context.
Hugging Face specifically identifies hallucinations and bias as important limitations of pretrained models. 
________________________________________
21. Hallucination
This is a must-know AI interview concept.
A hallucination occurs when a model generates an answer that sounds plausible but is unsupported or incorrect.
For example:
"According to a 2024 research paper by XYZ..."
The paper might not even exist.
Why?
Because the fundamental language-generation objective is not:
"Always tell the truth."
It is closer to:
"Generate a likely continuation given the context and learned parameters."
That's a huge distinction.
________________________________________
22. Why does hallucination happen?
Possible causes include:
•	incomplete training information 
•	noisy training data 
•	ambiguous prompts 
•	insufficient context 
•	distribution shift 
•	imperfect learned representations 
•	generation randomness 
•	no built-in guarantee of truth 
Therefore:
Fluent ≠ factual
This is one of the most important sentences to remember.
________________________________________
23. Bias
If training data contains biased patterns, the model may reproduce them.
The Hugging Face course demonstrates how pretrained models can reproduce problematic associations even when their training data appears relatively neutral. 
Therefore:
Data quality
      ↓
Learned patterns
      ↓
Model behavior
Poor or biased data can contribute to problematic outputs.
And fine-tuning doesn't automatically erase every underlying bias.
________________________________________
24. AI isn't magic
A very useful mental model is:
AI = mathematics + data + computation + optimization + architecture
Not magic.
A model's impressive behavior emerges from:
•	architecture 
•	training data 
•	parameters 
•	optimization 
•	scale 
•	context 
•	fine-tuning/alignment 
•	inference strategy 
•	sometimes external tools/retrieval 
