26. The complete picture you should remember
If your mentor suddenly says:
"Explain how an LLM works."
You should be able to say:
An LLM is a large neural network, commonly based on the Transformer architecture, trained on large amounts of data. Text is first converted into tokens, which are mapped to numerical embeddings. During pretraining, the model learns parameters by predicting tokens—often the next token in causal language modeling—and minimizing a loss using backpropagation and optimization. Transformers use self-attention to allow tokens to incorporate relevant contextual information. After pretraining, models can be fine-tuned or instruction-tuned for particular behaviors or tasks. During inference, the prompt is tokenized and processed, and the model generates output autoregressively by repeatedly predicting the next token. Despite their capabilities, LLMs can hallucinate, reproduce bias, lack required information, and make reasoning or factual errors, so important outputs need appropriate evaluation and verification.

